  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nSteady southwest winds along with an approaching axis of high \nclouds should limit further cooling overnight, with \ntemperatures remaining more or less where they are at, in the \nlower 30s (rural low spots) to lower 40 (Chicago and along the \nlakeshore). Winds are expected to become increasingly breezy \nprior to daybreak ahead of a weak cold front, owing to earlier \nmixing into a low-level jet overhead. Winds turn more westerly \nbehind the front later this morning, remaining breezy with\noccasional gusts up to 20-30 mph through sunset.\n\nUpper ridging quickly builds back across the Upper Midwest \ntoday bringing a return of above normal temperatures through the\nfirst part of the week along with continued dry conditions. \nExpect high temperatures in the upper 50s to around 60 today and\nMonday with low to mid 60s on Tuesday. Toward midweek, the \nnorthern stream upper jet begins to sag farther south which will\nallow a deepening shortwave trough to move across the area, \nbringing our next chance of showers (20-30 percent) on the north\nside of an associated developing surface low over central \nIllinois.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to support upper troughing and an \neventual closed upper low developing across the eastern CONUS \nlate in the week as a series of embedded shortwaves rotate over \nthe broader Great Lakes region. In addition to bringing a return\nof cooler (more seasonable) temperatures locally, there remains\na signal for lake effect precipitation developing over southern\nLake Michigan during the late Wednesday night through Friday\ntimeframe. The low-level flow orientation (and accordingly if \nand where the band sets up) will be dependent upon the strength \nand location of the deepening surface low to our east and the \nsurface high over the northern Plains. These details are \ndifficult to pin down this far out and will continue to monitor \ntrends over the next few days. An early look at model soundings \nindicate the window for sufficient saturation within the DGZ may\nbe very brief (if it occurs at all), which paired with the warm\nground temperatures would likely limit or prevent any snow \naccumulations. Nevertheless, it does look like some locations \nnear the lake could see their first snowflakes of the season.\n\nPetr",
